\chapter{A Vocabulary Shared by Both Backends}\label{ch:abstraction}

This chapter is the single file [[ViewerSession.swift]]. It names the backends and defines the
types that let the user interface work with either library: a status, a channel identifier, a
channel and a session protocol. None of them imports CloudXRKit or Foveated Streaming.

\section{Backends}

The enumeration names the backends and gives each a label for the picker. On iOS the foveated
case is called ``Streaming Session'' because an iPhone has no eye tracking and the stream is not
foveated there.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/ViewerSession.swift>>=
<<MIT permission notice>>
// Copyright (c) 2026 Pablo Figueroa and Claude.

import Foundation

/// The streaming stack used to reach the server.
///
/// `classic` is the CloudXRKit Swift package this viewer was built on. `foveated` is Apple's
/// Foveated Streaming system framework on visionOS (the headset shares the approximate region the
/// user looks at so the server spends its bits there) and Apple's StreamingSession package on iOS,
/// which exposes the same API without eye tracking.
enum StreamingBackend: String, CaseIterable {
    case classic
    case foveated

    var label: String {
        switch self {
        case .classic:
            "CloudXR"
#if os(visionOS)
        case .foveated:
            "Foveated Streaming"
#else
        case .foveated:
            "Streaming Session"
#endif
        }
    }

@
\label{sec:available}[[available]] is the list the
configuration form offers. Without [[FOVEATED_STREAMING]], that is in a simulator build, only the
classic backend exists, and the form hides the picker.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/ViewerSession.swift>>=
    /// The backends this build can use; simulator builds have no foveated backend.
    static var available: [StreamingBackend] {
#if FOVEATED_STREAMING
        allCases
#else
        [.classic]
#endif
    }
}

