\section{Status}

[[ViewerStatus]] is the union of the two libraries' state machines, reduced to what the views need.
CloudXRKit's [[authenticating]] and [[authenticated]] states fold into [[connecting]]; both
libraries' disconnection payloads become an optional error description, [[nil]] for a clean
disconnection. Two helpers answer the questions the views ask most.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/ViewerSession.swift>>=
/// Connection status shared by both backends, so the UI does not depend on either SDK's enum.
enum ViewerStatus: Equatable {
    case initialized
    case connecting
    case connected
    case pausing
    case paused
    case resuming
    case disconnecting
    /// `error` is nil when the disconnection was clean.
    case disconnected(error: String?)

    /// The session can start a new connection.
    var isIdle: Bool {
        switch self {
        case .initialized, .disconnected:
            true
        default:
            false
        }
    }

    var isDisconnected: Bool {
        if case .disconnected = self { true } else { false }
    }
}

@
[[ConnectionState]] pairs the status with its backend.
Observers compare consecutive values and ignore changes caused by switching backends
(Section~\ref{ch:app}).

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/ViewerSession.swift>>=
/// A status together with the backend that reported it, so observers can tell a real status
/// change from one caused by switching backends.
struct ConnectionState: Equatable {
    let backend: StreamingBackend
    let status: ViewerStatus
}

@
\section{Channels}

An opaque data channel is a bidirectional pipe for application messages between client and
server. CloudXRKit identifies a channel by a [[ChannelInfo]], Foveated Streaming by a
[[MessageChannel.ID]]. [[ViewerChannelID]] carries either one as an [[AnyHashable]] key, plus a
label for the picker; each adapter casts the key back to its own type.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/ViewerSession.swift>>=
/// Identifies an opaque data channel independently of the backend's own channel type.
struct ViewerChannelID: Hashable {
    /// Human-readable name for the channel picker.
    let label: String
    /// The backend's own identifier (`ChannelInfo` or `FoveatedStreamingSession.MessageChannel.ID`).
    let key: AnyHashable
}

@
A channel delivers messages as an asynchronous stream and sends with a throwing call.
CloudXRKit's [[MessageChannel]] reports a failed send by returning [[false]]; its adapter turns that
into [[ChannelSendError]].

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/ViewerSession.swift>>=
/// An open opaque data channel to the server.
protocol ViewerChannel: AnyObject {
    var receivedMessageStream: AsyncStream<Data> { get }
    var statusDescription: String { get }
    func send(_ data: Data) throws
}

struct ChannelSendError: LocalizedError {
    var errorDescription: String? { "The message could not be sent on this channel" }
}

@
\section{The session protocol}

[[ViewerSession]] is what the views use: status, a description to show, the channels, and the
lifecycle calls. The lifecycle calls are [[async]] because Foveated Streaming's are; CloudXRKit's
synchronous calls satisfy them trivially. The protocol is main-actor isolated, like
[[FoveatedStreamingSession]], so the foveated session can conform without any wrapper.

<<CloudXRViewer/Common/Streaming/ViewerSession.swift>>=
/// What the views need from a streaming session, whichever backend provides it.
@MainActor
protocol ViewerSession: AnyObject {
    var viewerStatus: ViewerStatus { get }
    /// The backend's own status text, shown under the configuration form.
    var statusDescription: String { get }
    var channelIDs: [ViewerChannelID] { get }
    func channel(for id: ViewerChannelID) -> (any ViewerChannel)?
    func disconnect() async
    func pause() async throws
    func resume() async throws
}
@
